<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warren Hastings</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1732">1732</a>–<a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Warren Hastings was the first Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, heading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1773 to 1785. He reorganised revenue and justice, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, and patronised oriental learning. On his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> he was impeached for corruption, and acquitted in 1795 after a seven-year trial.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warren-hastings.jpg" alt="Warren Hastings" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Joshua Reynolds · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warren_Hastings_by_Sir_Joshua_Reynolds.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Warren Hastings was born at Churchill, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a>, on 6 December 1732; his mother died soon after, and he was raised by his grandfather and then by an uncle, who sent him to Westminster School. He left at sixteen when his uncle died, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> as a writer in 1750. He learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Persian, and in 1752 was sent to the trading post at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasimbazar">Kasimbazar</a>. In 1756 he was imprisoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a>, escaped, and served as a volunteer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a>'s force that retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in January 1757.</p><p>In 1758 he became the British resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a>. Back on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> council from 1761, he protested against abuses of private trade, was overruled, and resigned in December 1764. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1769 as deputy ruler at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, and in 1772 became governor at Calcutta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The Regulating Act 1773 put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and made Hastings the first Governor-General, as one member of a five-man Supreme Council. According to William Dalrymple, he moved government from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, unified currency systems, ordered the codification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Muslim law, reformed the tax and customs system, backed James Rennell's survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and built granaries to guard against a repeat of the famine of 1770-71. In 1774 he sent troops to help the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a>, the first of several wars that harmed his reputation.</p><p>From 1779 to 1784 he faced a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, with a French fleet under Admiral Suffren also in the field. He made peace with the Marathas in 1782 and broke up the coalition. He raised funds from local treasuries, which later became the main line of attack against him. In 1781 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a> 'Aliya at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hastings resigned in 1785 and was replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-cornwallis-1st-marquess-cornwallis">Lord Cornwallis</a>. Impeached by the House of Commons in 1787 over embezzlement, extortion and coercion, he was tried before the House of Lords from 13 February 1788, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a> among the prosecutors. The trial took 148 days over seven years, and he was acquitted on 24 April 1795, though he complained of being bankrupted by its cost. He was made a privy councillor in 1814 and died on 22 August 1818.</p><p>His introduction to Charles Wilkins's translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, dated 4 October 1784, argued that knowledge of a conquered people was useful to the state. Historians note that his reliance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> advisers to interpret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> law helped solidify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> privileges.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Warren Hastings” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1773 <b>Warren Hastings</b> appointed a resident, Nathaniel Middleton, at Lucknow to shape Oudh's policy and direct its internal affairs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1773 it was the de facto ruler of much of the Lower Gangetic Plain, and that year it abolished local rule in Bengal and made Calcutta its capital, with <b>Warren Hastings</b> as the first Governor-General.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles Wilkins published the first English translation in 1785, with an introduction by <b>Warren Hastings</b>, and French (1787), German and Russian versions followed.</blockquote><a href="/work/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Chait Singh's resistance to <b>Warren Hastings's</b> heavy demands led to his rising and flight in 1781, a key charge in Hastings's later impeachment.</blockquote><a href="/state/benares-state">Benares State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Churchill, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Daylesford, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.92,-1.59&amp;year=1787" aria-label="Churchill on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Warren Hastings"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4916 5688h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4916 5688h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.92,-1.59&amp;year=1787">What was here in 1787? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
